AWS-011 — Workflow Exceptions

Document ID: AWS-011
Title: Workflow Exceptions
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Workflow Exception Model (CWEM-X) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Workflow Exception Model establishes the principles, governance, structures, and lifecycle for managing approved deviations from Workflow Definitions and Workflow Executions while preserving engineering integrity, operational continuity, accountability, and traceability.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Workflow exception architecture
Exception requests
Operational risk assessment
Compensating workflows
Exception approval
Monitoring
Recovery
Exception lifecycle

This specification applies to every governed deviation from Workflow Definitions or Workflow Instances.

3. Canonical Definition

A Workflow Exception is a governed, explicitly authorized, and time-bounded deviation from the normal execution of a Workflow Definition or Workflow Instance under defined operational conditions while preserving accountability, traceability, and engineering intent.

Workflow Exceptions modify execution behavior.

They SHALL NOT modify Workflow Definitions.

4. Objectives

The Workflow Exception Model SHALL:

Govern execution deviations.
Preserve workflow integrity.
Enable operational flexibility.
Minimize execution risk.
Preserve traceability.
Support recovery.
Maintain governance.
Prevent undocumented execution changes.
5. Exception Principles

Every Workflow Exception SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Explicit Authorization

Workflow exceptions SHALL require explicit approval unless an emergency execution profile explicitly permits immediate activation with retrospective approval.

Temporary Validity

Every Workflow Exception SHALL define an effective period.

Permanent execution changes SHALL be modeled through workflow revision rather than exceptions.

Operational Risk Awareness

Every exception SHALL include an operational risk assessment.

Compensating Execution

Where execution integrity is affected, compensating workflows or compensating activities SHALL be defined.

Traceability

Every exception SHALL remain permanently traceable.

Recoverability

Every active exception SHALL define a recovery path returning execution to governed operation whenever feasible.

6. Canonical Exception Structure

Every Workflow Exception SHALL define:

Property	Description
Exception ID	Globally unique identifier
Workflow Reference	Affected Workflow Definition or Instance
Exception Type	Classification
Requester	Initiating authority
Approver	Approval authority
Effective Period	Validity interval
Risk Rating	Operational risk
Current Status	Lifecycle state
7. Workflow Exception Categories

Atlas defines the following canonical categories.

Category	Description
Activity Exception	Skip, repeat, or replace an activity
Participant Exception	Temporary participant substitution
Timing Exception	Deadline or schedule modification
Resource Exception	Resource unavailability
Dependency Exception	Dependency bypass
System Exception	System outage or degraded service
Emergency Execution	Emergency operational response
AI Exception	AI execution restriction or override

Additional categories MAY be introduced through governed profiles.

8. Exception Request

Every request SHALL define:

Request identifier
Workflow reference
Affected activities
Justification
Requested duration
Operational impact
Supporting evidence
Proposed compensating execution

Requests SHALL remain independently identifiable.

9. Operational Risk Assessment

Every Workflow Exception SHALL assess:

Engineering impact
Safety impact
Operational impact
Compliance impact
Schedule impact
Financial impact
Security impact
Probability and severity

Risk SHALL be explicitly documented.

10. Compensating Workflows

A Workflow Exception MAY require one or more Compensating Workflows.

Examples include:

Additional inspection
Secondary approval
Manual validation
Recovery execution
Supplemental testing
Independent review
Data reconciliation

Compensating Workflows SHALL themselves be governed Workflow Definitions.

11. Exception Approval

Every approval SHALL define:

Approval authority
Decision timestamp
Approval rationale
Applicable policies
Operational conditions
Review schedule

Approvals SHALL remain immutable.

12. Exception Lifecycle

Every Workflow Exception SHALL progress through:

